  "query_preview": "..\nNoticeably warmer (+5-10F vs. last night) and more humid \novernight/early morning across CPA with a couple of showers and \nstorms over the northwest and southeast portions of the forecast\narea. Cluster of showers/storms across the NW Alleghenies are \nbeing driven by a lead shortwave trough crossing Lake Erie, \nwhile the more spotty activity over the mid to lower Susq Valley\nis fueled more by low level WAA and SWly LLJ. Some valley fog \nis evident on IR satellite in the interior central valleys, but \ncoverage is significantly less than Wednesday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms \npossible this afternoon into tonight\n\nScattered thunderstorm development is expected Thursday\nafternoon ahead of a cold front across CPA. SPC has continued \nto expand the level 2/5 severe t-storm risk which now reaches \nfarther west to the eastern slopes of the Allegheny Mtns. \n\nModerate destabilization is fcst into the afternoon along/ahead\nof a weak surface trough/front extending from central NY\nsouthward through central PA. Scattered thunderstorm development\nis expected by mid to late afternoon, with sufficient deep layer \nflow/shear for some storm organization. The strongest cells or \nclusters will be capable of producing scattered damaging winds \nand at least isolated hail. Modestly curved low-level hodographs\nmay also support some brief-tornado potential. The severe \nthreat should persist into the evening before storms weaken/exit\nthe eastern part of the CWA. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Flooding downpours possible this afternoon into \ntonight across portions of the Lower Susquehanna Valley \n\nA healthy influx of moisture via southwest flow will increase\nPWATs to ~1.5\" across the Lower Susquehanna Valley by this\nafternoon. CAMs indicate potential for 2-4\" spot rain amounts\n(via HREF PMM) within zone of low level convergence along weak \npre frontal/lee sfc trough. A couple rounds of heavy t-storm \ndownpours are possible with rain rates 1-2\"/hr that could result\nin scattered areas of flash flooding. Based on the +RA signal in\nthe hires ensembles and coordination with WFO PHI, we issued a\n[flash] flood watch for Schuylkill, Lebanon, and Lancaster\nCounties. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n\nVariable sky conditions for the lunar eclipse tonight: \nForecast viewing conditions for the lunar eclipse range from \nfair to poor Thursday night across central PA. The eclipse will \nbegin at 10:34 pm EDT and last for about 3 hours, ending at 1:52\nam EDT Friday. The worst view is most likely over the eastern \nhalf of CPA due to exiting storms and associated cloud cover. \nThe eclipse will take place during August's full moon, commonly \nreferred to as the Sturgeon Moon. It will also be the last full \nmoon of the summer with the next full moon set to rise a few \nnights after the autumnal (Fall) equinox on September 22nd. \n\nA drying trend is forecast Friday into the last weekend of \nAugust, thanks to high pressure building into the area behind \nthe cold front. This will likely setup a couple of foggy \nmornings (Friday and Saturday). Rainfall looks fairly limited \nheading into the first week of September; however despite the \nstart of meteorological Fall the summer is not over with temps \nexpected to rise above the historical average into the 80-90 \ndegree range.",
